@@s043 recovery
@@s044 Dates from the LearnIT page of the course. Every Thursday from 11 to 12 the TA session serves to solve the Kattis problem of the week.
@@s045 Collaboration: what is allowed
@@s046 You can discuss with classmates and the TAs ("I've solved XYZ using ABC's algorithm, just make sure that Foo is always Baa"). You cannot publish code, have the exercise solved by others or by an <b>artificial intelligence</b>, nor pass off someone else's code as your own. If someone helped you, write it in a comment in the code you hand in ("Thanks to Alice Cooper for pointing me to…").
@@s047 In your repo
@@s048 In the folder <code>Algorithm Design</code> there are your solutions to introductory exercises (Echo, Alice, Backspace, BaseP, Bto01), exhaustive search (Perket, Satisfaction), stable matching (Draft, and Matching still to finish), greedy (Greedy) and closest pair (PairPoints). The ones I have run give correct results; the "accepted" status, however, you only see on Kattis.
@@s049 Red Scare
@@s050 The text is in the repo <code>thorehusfeldt/algdes-labs</code> on GitHub (folder <code>red-scare</code>).
@@s051 It is done in the <b>delivery group</b>, about 5 people, and handed in on LearnIT: code and <b>report</b>. It must not be discussed on the public forum.
@@s052 "This exercise is about choosing between and using known algorithmic paradigms. It is not about implementing these paradigms": you may reuse code for known algorithms or take it from a library, declaring it in the report.
@@s053 About three weeks of work. On 12 and 19 November the TA sessions are group feedback on the project (18 minutes per group): come with ideas on <i>Few</i>, <i>Alternate</i> and <i>None</i>.
@@s054 <b>Delivery: Friday 27 November, 23:59.</b> Feedback by 3 December; if needed, a new delivery by 10 December.
@@s055 It is the training for the exam
@@s056 Red Scare asks you to decide, for several questions on the same graph, which paradigm to use: BFS, DP on a DAG, flow, or "is NP-hard". It is exactly the <a href="triage.html">classification</a> work of the exam, on a bigger problem.
